1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
like the Everglades, but with multiple roots going in.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:04,800
And we're like, that's weird.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,240
But anyway, normal trees have one root,

4
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:09,680
and then they spread out from there.

5
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:13,080
That's the idea of extensive forms.

6
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:16,000
So a simple example could be your career game tree.

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,080
So each node, you can choose, you can keep going to school,

8
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:20,960
or you can get a job and get your payout.

9
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:23,080
So this would be a one-person game

10
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:25,120
where there's two choices.

11
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:29,040
You can drop out or go on.

12
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:32,240
If you drop out, then you might get your high school dropout

13
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:33,160
wage.

14
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:36,240
And then if you go on, then you'll

15
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:40,240
have the further choice of stop or go on.

16
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:42,440
And then if you stop, then you'll get your high school

17
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:47,520
wage, or you go on, and then you could go and stop and go on.

18
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:52,320
Go on, and then you could either get the college wage,

19
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:55,120
or you keep going on to your PhD, say.

20
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:56,440
And this is go on.

21
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:59,120
Let's see what I'm doing here.

22
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:01,360
Let's see here.

23
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:03,120
That's high school dropout.

24
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:04,680
That is finished high school.

25
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:06,240
That's finished college.

26
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:09,840
And yes, actually, you could just stop there.

27
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:12,080
Or you could go on to that, say, your PhD.

28
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:14,480
And then we could add on with getting a second PhD,

29
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:15,880
and that would be worse.

30
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:20,160
So you get exactly one PhD, no more, no less.

31
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:23,600
Anyone getting a second PhD right now?

32
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:26,240
When I was getting my PhD, there was

33
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:29,120
a guy there who had actually not only already had a PhD in math,

34
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:31,520
he had been a math professor.

35
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:34,880
And we're there taking PhD microwave with a math

36
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:37,080
professor.

37
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:39,640
But he was saying, I could barely

38
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:41,640
get any kind of decent job as a math professor.

39
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:44,440
Whereas here, I'm just like a titan towering over you.

40
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:48,920
So you guys don't understand any of this stuff, do you?

41
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:54,720
So that is a case of extensive form

42
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:56,560
where you've only got one player.

43
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:58,400
Now, a more interesting case, there

44
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:01,320
is a movie from the 70s with Gene Hackman called

45
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:03,040
The French Connection, where there's

46
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:06,000
a policeman who is strangely named Popeye.

47
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,520
I don't, and I still am confused by why he's named Popeye.

48
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:12,120
Why did someone write the story like this?

49
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:13,640
Where they say, well, no one's going

50
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:16,040
to confuse Gene Hackman, the policeman,

51
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,560
with the cartoon character of Popeye.

52
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:20,760
Some of you don't even know who Popeye is.

53
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:21,880
You know who the person is?

54
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:22,800
Popeye.

55
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:23,920
All right.

56
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:27,560
Anyway, so there's a character, a policeman named Popeye.

57
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:29,600
And he's hunting a criminal in New York.

58
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:32,400
And the criminal runs onto a subway.

59
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:34,360
And then Popeye gets onto the subway.

60
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:36,200
But then the criminal gets off the subway.

61
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:37,200
Then Popeye gets off.

62
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:39,320
And then they just sit there looking at each other.

63
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:41,800
Because Popeye wants to be on the subway with the criminal

64
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:43,520
to find out what's going on.

65
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:46,560
And the criminal does not want to be on the same subway,

66
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:48,560
be on the same place as Popeye.

67
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,840
Because the criminal does not want the cop

68
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:53,480
to know what is going on.

69
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:56,040
So you can think about it as an extensive form

70
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:58,560
where we start off with, let's see,

71
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:03,120
with the criminal decides to be on or off of the subway.

72
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:05,400
And then Popeye decides to be on or off.

73
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:12,920
And then when we're thinking about the payoffs,

74
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:14,760
so the criminal is the first actor.

75
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:17,080
So the criminal is their number one.

76
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:20,160
And the cop, Popeye, is number two.

77
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:25,080
So if they were both on, then which person

78
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:27,800
is going to be getting the low payoff?

79
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:28,400
The criminal.

80
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:29,760
Yeah, the criminal's getting the low payoff.

81
00:03:29,760 --> 00:03:30,640
Let's call that zero.

82
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:32,600
And the policeman's getting the good one of 10.

83
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:34,240
He's happy.

84
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:35,600
But then there's the other part.

85
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:37,680
So if they're both on, on the other hand,

86
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:41,600
if the criminal is on the subway and the cop is off the subway,

87
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:45,000
then the criminal is happy and the cop is unhappy.

88
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,440
If the criminal is off and the cop is on,

89
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:49,040
then it's the same thing.

90
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:51,040
So the criminal is happy and the cop is unhappy.

91
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:52,720
And if they're both off, then it is

92
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:56,960
the cop that is happy and the criminal that is unhappy.

93
00:03:56,960 --> 00:04:01,200
So we have all of those possibilities.

94
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:02,700
Now, there's a bunch of complications

95
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:05,640
about how you would deal with extensive forms.

96
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:09,040
So one of them is, what about nature?

97
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:11,080
What if there's also a possibility

98
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:13,960
that lightning strikes the subway?

99
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:15,920
And how does that change the game?

100
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:18,880
And the way that you normally model nature

101
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:23,040
is just put it in as a player that plays everything randomly.

102
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:25,880
So sometimes you actually put nature in as a player.

103
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:27,960
And then you'll say, well, either we're

104
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:30,840
in state of the world one or state of the world two

105
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:32,360
randomly.

106
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:35,800
And so if nature is a player, why does it act randomly?

107
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:37,300
Well, it would have to be that nature

108
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:39,000
has the same payoffs in every situation,

109
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,240
so it's indifferent between all outcomes, which

110
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:44,000
is just a philosophical claim.

111
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,080
The universe doesn't care about you.

112
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:48,960
It's either one way or the other.

113
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:52,160
Now, there's also an issue with information sets.

114
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:56,720
And that is, well, what about simultaneous moves?

115
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:59,040
And the way that it is handled with extensive forms

116
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:01,460
is to say, well, there's really no difference

117
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:05,920
between moving simultaneously and moving sequentially

118
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:09,440
without knowing what your opponent did.

119
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:10,320
So think about this.

120
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:12,960
Suppose we either play rock, paper, scissors sequentially,

121
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:15,720
where we both play and we reveal at the same time.

122
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:19,480
Or you could go and secretly write down

123
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,040
what you want on your strategy.

124
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:22,880
And then I go, and then I, without seeing

125
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:24,800
what you wrote down, announce what I'm doing,

126
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:26,840
and then you open up the paper.

127
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:29,920
For all practical purposes, those are the same thing.

128
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:33,880
Either we play simultaneously or we play sequentially,

129
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:36,320
but I don't know what you did.

130
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:41,000
So the way that we handle this simultaneous play

131
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,600
with extensive forms is simply to say,

132
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,840
that's just the same as making a decision without information.

133
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:51,560
So then it's conventional to go and draw a dotted box

134
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:57,040
around different places if you can't tell

135
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:58,880
the difference between them.

136
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:00,560
So if you don't know, did he do rock?

137
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:01,320
Did he do paper?

138
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:02,640
Or did he do scissors?

139
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:05,080
If you don't know what he did, then rather than saying

140
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:06,120
that you know what node you're at,

141
00:06:06,120 --> 00:06:08,400
we just draw a dotted line around all the nodes

142
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:11,080
that you can't distinguish from each other,

143
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:14,360
and then play strategically from there.

144
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:17,680
So simultaneous moves are equivalent to sequential moves

145
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:19,640
with uncertainty, which means that we

146
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:22,280
can keep the nice tree format even if people

147
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:23,480
are moving at the same time.

148
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:25,200
Because if you, because otherwise it

149
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:26,960
would be hard to understand what do you do if people

150
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:27,880
move at the same time.

151
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:29,440
How can you show that in a tree?

152
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:33,880
The answer is we just treat that as if everything is sequential,

153
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:36,680
but sometimes you don't know what's happening.

154
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:39,120
And then we sort of spray paint some of the nodes

155
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:41,760
to show that you don't know where you are in the tree,

156
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:43,720
but it still looks like a tree.

157
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:47,560
You just don't know where in the tree you are.

158
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:49,920
All right, so extensive forms are done occasionally,

159
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:51,640
especially for simple games.

160
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:55,320
But it is more common for economists

161
00:06:55,320 --> 00:06:58,520
to use what are called moral forms.

162
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:00,840
Someone write on that?

163
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:02,080
Yes, someone write on that?

164
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:05,040
Oh, that is, yes.

165
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:06,000
All right.

166
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:07,320
I didn't do it.

167
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:09,320
If you draw over it with the whiteboard marker,

168
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:10,000
it should erase.

169
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:10,500
Yeah.

170
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:14,720
It's mine.

171
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:16,080
As long as it stays wet.

172
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:17,120
Ah.

173
00:07:17,120 --> 00:07:19,560
Oh, wow.

174
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:20,920
All right.

175
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:22,640
Amazing.

176
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:24,520
How could that be?

177
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:26,120
You broke the rules of the simulation.

178
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:28,360
Yeah.

179
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:30,680
It's a solid.

180
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:31,760
It's a legitimate change.

181
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:32,800
Does this work generally?

182
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:37,360
Can you get rid of stains on clothes the same way?

183
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:40,320
Mark on it with a marker.

184
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:41,240
All right.

185
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:45,360
Well, before I throw something out, I'll try it.

186
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:48,720
OK, so now the other way of value-driven games

187
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:53,440
is what is called a moral form, also called a strategic form.

188
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:59,200
Same concept, different word, different phrase.

189
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:01,600
So what you do here is you display a complete grid

190
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:04,400
of strategy profiles and payoffs.

191
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:07,400
It's a complete grid of strategy profiles and payoffs.

192
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:10,640
And the grid is one dimension per player.

193
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:14,520
As you might guess, then, very often used for two-player games

194
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:16,360
because it's really hard to go and draw

195
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:19,600
three-dimensional grids.

196
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:21,520
So if you just imagine what it would be like,

197
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:23,080
so you'd have to actually be putting the payoffs

198
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:24,120
in this three-dimensional grid.

199
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:26,520
And then four-dimensional grids are really hard to draw.

200
00:08:26,520 --> 00:08:30,200
So when people do things like that,

201
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:32,440
normally they'll just go and give you the equations,

202
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:38,280
and they'll give up on actually trying to draw the grid.

203
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:40,240
So the grid is one dimension per player.

204
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:43,680
And the interesting thing about moral forms and moral forms

205
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:46,800
is that they will often contain irrelevant information

206
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:49,880
about what you would have done in situations that would never

207
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:53,840
happen, even where you ended up, which is strange at first.

208
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:57,640
But there is actually good reason for doing this.

209
00:08:57,640 --> 00:09:00,200
And as we're going to see, actually,

210
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:02,840
your behavior in situations that don't occur

211
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:04,800
can actually strategically change

212
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:08,480
what happens in situations that do occur, which at first

213
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:09,760
sounds totally counterintuitive.

214
00:09:09,760 --> 00:09:12,560
But I'm going to try to make it intuitive as we go on.

215
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:19,800
So let's think about normal form of your one-player career game.

216
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:21,120
There could just be, I don't think

217
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:21,960
I'll even bother writing it down.

218
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:24,080
So if you drop out before high school,

219
00:09:24,080 --> 00:09:25,360
that could be a payoff of 10.

220
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:27,240
Finish high school and stuff, 15.

221
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:29,400
Finish your bachelor's degree and stuff, then 20.

222
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:31,320
Finish your PhD and stuff, then 30.

223
00:09:31,320 --> 00:09:34,000
And finish two PhDs and stuff, that would be zero.

224
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:37,560
And then, of course, what you should do is get one PhD.

225
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:38,080
Exactly.

226
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:40,280
Now, number of PhDs exactly equal to one.

227
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:42,160
All right, now for the French connection game

228
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:43,400
gets a little more interesting.

229
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:50,760
So there, again, we can show criminal, followed by the cop.

230
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:55,040
And then we go and we show, here's the third possibility.

231
00:09:55,040 --> 00:09:59,880
So they can go on or off, on or off.

232
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:02,120
And then we just go and put down what the payoffs are.

233
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:06,560
So 0, 10, 10, 0, 10, 0, 0, 10.

234
00:10:09,560 --> 00:10:12,160
Now, let's see here.

235
00:10:12,160 --> 00:10:14,040
Now, an important thing is player one

236
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:16,200
is always shown on the left.

237
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:18,160
This, of course, is not a law of nature.

238
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:20,360
But it is a convention that is universally followed.

239
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:24,760
So you should learn it, too, so that no one is confused.

240
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:27,360
So I was teaching game theory to my 14-year-olds.

241
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:29,960
And one of them did switch the axes.

242
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:32,080
And then, well, he said, well, why can't I do that?

243
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:34,800
Well, you can, but you will confuse everyone else

244
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:35,400
who knows this.

245
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:40,480
So you just need to do it the conventional way for clarity.

246
00:10:40,480 --> 00:10:42,360
So anyway, this is always player one there.

247
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:44,240
That's always player two.

248
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:46,920
Every time I've ever seen it, it's been that way.

249
00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:50,560
So you should do it the same way.

250
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:52,480
All right, now there's an example for preps.

251
00:10:52,480 --> 00:10:57,080
So here he has player one chooses capital A or capital D.

252
00:10:57,080 --> 00:10:59,480
And then he plays D, the game ends.

253
00:10:59,480 --> 00:11:02,200
If he plays capital A, then player two

254
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:06,480
chooses alpha or delta, alpha, alpha, or delta.

255
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:08,400
And if he plays delta, the game ends.

256
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:10,880
And if he plays alpha, player one chooses little a or little

257
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:11,360
d.

258
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:12,920
And then either way, the game ends.

259
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:15,680
And then we can write down the normal form.

260
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:17,800
And notice this normal form is going

261
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:21,440
to do what I mentioned, which is include information

262
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:24,320
about irrelevant possibilities.

263
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:26,920
So here we've got all the possible combinations

264
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:30,480
of capital A, little a, capital A, little d, capital D,

265
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:33,480
little a, capital D, little d.

266
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:36,040
And those are all player one's options.

267
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:39,920
And then here we've got alpha and delta.

268
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:42,600
And here we have 3, 1, 4, 3.

269
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:49,160
2, 3, 4, 3, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, and 1, 2.

270
00:11:49,160 --> 00:11:50,160
All right.

271
00:11:50,160 --> 00:11:54,480
So in terms of common sense, you might say, well, look.

272
00:11:54,480 --> 00:11:56,840
If he plays capital D, the game ends.

273
00:11:56,840 --> 00:11:59,560
So what does it mean to say that he would have done a or b

274
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:02,560
or a, little a or little d in a situation where he never

275
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:04,960
does those things?

276
00:12:04,960 --> 00:12:07,960
And that is an important question, which we'll come to.

277
00:12:07,960 --> 00:12:09,040
But never mind.

278
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:12,680
And that is an important question, which we'll come to.

279
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:15,640
But notice that the normal form includes that information.

280
00:12:15,640 --> 00:12:23,320
So every possible scenario, so what's the right way?

281
00:12:23,320 --> 00:12:25,760
Even things that could never happen given earlier choice,

282
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:30,200
still we go and write them down in the grid for completeness.

283
00:12:30,200 --> 00:12:33,840
Now, is this purely a mathematical fetishism thing

284
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:35,720
where we go in, which you wouldn't put it past

285
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:37,320
mathematicians, just saying.

286
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:39,640
We need to cover all our basics, including the basics that

287
00:12:39,640 --> 00:12:42,680
cannot be occupied.

288
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:44,520
But no, actually, it turns out there's

289
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:47,760
some economic intuition to doing this, which I think

290
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:51,920
we'll be able to get to around the end of today.

291
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:53,080
All right.

292
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:55,080
All right, so you can look at the situation now.

293
00:12:55,080 --> 00:12:58,600
From this, can you write down the sense of form?

294
00:12:58,600 --> 00:13:00,720
And of course, the answer is yes.

295
00:13:00,720 --> 00:13:02,520
All right, so as I said, so player one

296
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:06,600
chooses a, capital A or capital D. And he plays D,

297
00:13:06,600 --> 00:13:07,720
the game ends.

298
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:09,080
Now, based upon this normal form,

299
00:13:09,080 --> 00:13:13,840
what are the payoffs from over there?

300
00:13:13,840 --> 00:13:15,760
What happens?

301
00:13:15,760 --> 00:13:17,880
What always happens when capital D gets played?

302
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:18,560
1, 2.

303
00:13:18,560 --> 00:13:20,040
1, 2, yes.

304
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:21,400
So 1, 2.

305
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:23,120
All right, and then if you play capital A,

306
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:26,400
then there's alpha or delta possibilities.

307
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:29,120
And again, if you play delta, the game ends.

308
00:13:29,120 --> 00:13:30,960
So what happens then?

309
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:35,040
So what are the payoffs if the first person plays A,

310
00:13:35,040 --> 00:13:37,000
second person plays delta?

311
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:37,680
4, 3.

312
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:39,880
So we got that.

313
00:13:39,880 --> 00:13:41,920
And then finally, for this, then there's

314
00:13:41,920 --> 00:13:44,280
capital A or little a, little d.

315
00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:48,240
And then for those, we've got, so A, alpha, A.

316
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:52,080
So that's 3, 1, and 2, 3.

317
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:56,320
So that is the sense of form of that normal form.

318
00:13:56,320 --> 00:13:57,320
OK.

319
00:13:57,320 --> 00:13:59,000
All right, questions?

320
00:13:59,000 --> 00:13:59,500
Yes?

321
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:02,920
So how do we know if the players play simultaneously?

322
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:05,560
So the answer is, if we don't actually

323
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:10,480
put those dotted lines around two or more of the nodes,

324
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:12,240
they play sequentially.

325
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:16,200
Is what we're seeing here.

326
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:20,920
So in other words, if you didn't know where you were,

327
00:14:20,920 --> 00:14:25,960
then we'd have to go and put dotted lines here around them.

328
00:14:25,960 --> 00:14:27,840
OK, all right.

329
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:30,240
Now, all that we've done so far is

330
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:34,000
we've learned some vocabulary, some notation.

331
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,240
So none of this actually tells us anything

332
00:14:36,240 --> 00:14:38,200
about the real world so far.

333
00:14:38,200 --> 00:14:44,120
Now we get to the first answer to a really big question that

334
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:46,280
has many answers.

335
00:14:46,280 --> 00:14:48,400
There are many answers to this question.

336
00:14:48,400 --> 00:14:50,200
But the first answer to the question,

337
00:14:50,200 --> 00:14:54,160
so what does game theory say that people actually do?

338
00:14:54,160 --> 00:14:54,960
All right.

339
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:56,840
Well, normally in game theory, people

340
00:14:56,840 --> 00:14:59,680
begin trying to solve a game using the weakest

341
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:02,400
possible assumptions and see if they get an answer.

342
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:04,560
And if they get an answer, then they say, all right,

343
00:15:04,560 --> 00:15:07,000
let's test to see whether that answer is true.

344
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,440
If they can't get an answer using weak assumptions,

345
00:15:09,440 --> 00:15:11,760
then they strengthen the assumptions a bit

346
00:15:11,760 --> 00:15:13,640
and then see if they get an answer

347
00:15:13,640 --> 00:15:16,040
and then test if that assumption is true.

348
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:18,000
And so on.

349
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,560
So start with some relatively weak assumptions,

350
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:24,640
then strengthen them until a plausible answer emerges.

351
00:15:24,640 --> 00:15:28,480
Now, the weakest assumption that game theorists use.

352
00:15:28,480 --> 00:15:32,320
The weakest assumption is that nobody

353
00:15:32,320 --> 00:15:34,960
plays what are called strictly dominated strategies.

354
00:15:42,960 --> 00:15:46,240
So slash through it.

355
00:15:46,240 --> 00:15:48,080
Nobody does those.

356
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:51,000
All right, that is the first and weakest assumption

357
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,840
that game theorists use to try to solve models in game theory.

358
00:15:54,840 --> 00:15:57,480
So what is a strictly dominated strategy?

359
00:15:57,480 --> 00:15:59,040
A strictly dominated strategy is one

360
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:02,760
that is worse for you in all conditions of the world.

361
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:05,160
No matter what your opponents do,

362
00:16:05,160 --> 00:16:07,240
if a strategy is worse for you than another one,

363
00:16:07,240 --> 00:16:09,280
that strategy is strictly dominated.

364
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:11,040
And the assumption is that people

365
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:13,720
don't play that strategy.

366
00:16:13,720 --> 00:16:15,680
All right, that sounds really reasonable, right?

367
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:17,640
If there's something that is always a bad idea,

368
00:16:17,640 --> 00:16:20,360
no one will ever do it.

369
00:16:20,360 --> 00:16:25,160
So of course, if you know any psychology or psychology

370
00:16:25,160 --> 00:16:29,120
economics, you will know some counter examples.

371
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:32,160
But people do do things that are bad for them no matter what.

372
00:16:32,160 --> 00:16:36,400
So in any case, we're just starting out in a simple place.

373
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:40,080
All right, so people don't play strictly dominated strategies.

374
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:42,240
And so if there's a strategy that is worse for you,

375
00:16:42,240 --> 00:16:44,840
no matter what your opponent does, you don't play it.

376
00:16:44,840 --> 00:16:47,680
Now, the way you get more mileage out of this

377
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:50,840
is that if nobody plays strictly dominated strategies,

378
00:16:50,840 --> 00:16:53,680
then sometimes once you realize, well,

379
00:16:53,680 --> 00:16:56,840
that guy will never play that strategy, then you'll say, ah.

380
00:16:56,840 --> 00:16:59,600
So then actually, I can eliminate something else

381
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:01,680
because I know that he'll never do that thing that

382
00:17:01,680 --> 00:17:02,680
is always bad for him.

383
00:17:02,680 --> 00:17:05,520
So I can ignore it when I figure out what to do.

384
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:08,880
And I'll act as if that possibility doesn't even exist.

385
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:11,960
And then I will perhaps be able to eliminate some strategies,

386
00:17:11,960 --> 00:17:14,480
which might mean that he'll be able to eliminate some more.

387
00:17:14,480 --> 00:17:16,320
And then I'll be able to eliminate some more.

388
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:19,960
And so on, it may be that we wind up with only one thing

389
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:21,480
that we do, right?

390
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:24,000
Even though on the surface, it looks really complicated,

391
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,960
it might not actually be very complicated after a while

392
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:29,400
when we really play through.

393
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:30,080
OK.

394
00:17:30,080 --> 00:17:34,000
Now, if eliminating all the strictly dominated strategies

395
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,120
finally leaves you with only one equilibrium,

396
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:37,640
then we have a special name for that.

397
00:17:37,640 --> 00:17:40,160
We say that the game is dominant solvable.

398
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:41,880
It's dominant solvable.

399
00:17:41,880 --> 00:17:44,880
The classic example, of course, is the prisoner's lemma.

400
00:17:44,880 --> 00:17:47,560
Everyone here has seen the prisoner's lemma at some point.

401
00:17:47,560 --> 00:17:49,520
So the whole idea of the prisoner's lemma,

402
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:52,400
or there's many versions, but the classic version is,

403
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:55,080
the policeman has two suspects for a crime.

404
00:17:55,080 --> 00:17:57,320
And he tells each of them, look, you can either

405
00:17:57,320 --> 00:17:59,280
confess or not confess.

406
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:04,120
If you confess and your partner doesn't,

407
00:18:04,120 --> 00:18:05,880
then you're going to get a light sentence.

408
00:18:05,880 --> 00:18:08,960
And your partner, rather, is going to get a heavy sentence.

409
00:18:08,960 --> 00:18:10,760
If he confesses and you don't, then you're

410
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:11,720
going to get a heavy sentence.

411
00:18:11,720 --> 00:18:13,320
And he's going to get off lightly.

412
00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:14,920
If neither of you confesses, then I'll

413
00:18:14,920 --> 00:18:16,640
go and pin on a small crime to you.

414
00:18:16,640 --> 00:18:18,960
You'll both get a traffic ticket or something.

415
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:22,080
But if both of you confess, then you both get a harsh penalty,

416
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:24,320
although not quite as harsh as being

417
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:26,400
the person that refused to testify when

418
00:18:26,400 --> 00:18:29,560
your partner did testify.

419
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:31,800
So it's all based upon the criminal justice system.

420
00:18:31,800 --> 00:18:33,920
That's why it's called the prisoner's lemma.

421
00:18:33,920 --> 00:18:36,960
And notice where the apostrophe comes.

422
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:39,680
Is it before the S or after the S?

423
00:18:39,680 --> 00:18:42,760
It's after to show it is a lemma that collectively belongs

424
00:18:42,760 --> 00:18:44,720
to the two prisoners.

425
00:18:44,720 --> 00:18:50,000
And the prisoners are in a difficult strategic situation.

426
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:53,880
Right now, there's a very strong intuition

427
00:18:53,880 --> 00:18:57,160
that if the prisoners can just talk and say, don't talk, OK,

428
00:18:57,160 --> 00:19:00,560
no way I would ever talk, that this changes the game.

429
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:04,440
But in terms of the actual payoffs and the logic of the game,

430
00:19:04,440 --> 00:19:06,040
it doesn't change it at all.

431
00:19:06,040 --> 00:19:08,720
We think that talking would change the outcome.

432
00:19:08,720 --> 00:19:10,760
By the way, is there experimental evidence that

433
00:19:10,760 --> 00:19:12,880
talking does change outcomes?

434
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:14,200
Yeah, there is.

435
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:17,320
But it's not because of the economics or the game theory.

436
00:19:17,320 --> 00:19:19,320
It's because of psychology.

437
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:22,600
It's because when people talk, this changes the way they feel.

438
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:26,480
It's not because it changes what is actually in your interest.

439
00:19:26,480 --> 00:19:29,440
Because again, in terms of the actual payoffs

440
00:19:29,440 --> 00:19:33,040
in the prisoner's lemma, after you say,

441
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:36,080
I promise I would never betray you, so don't betray me, OK?

442
00:19:36,080 --> 00:19:37,040
OK, brother.

443
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:39,360
And then as soon as you leave the room, say, all right,

444
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:41,840
you totally did it.

445
00:19:41,840 --> 00:19:47,840
And that is, again, in terms of the game theory, what happens.

446
00:19:47,840 --> 00:19:50,840
So part of it's that sentimental commitment that you've made.

447
00:19:50,840 --> 00:19:54,920
I think you're also identifying the probability

448
00:19:54,920 --> 00:19:56,240
that these boxes are different.

449
00:19:56,240 --> 00:19:57,760
Well, in other words, you could also

450
00:19:57,760 --> 00:20:00,160
say you're just identifying whether the person underneath


